\section{Introduction}\label{sec:staging-intro}

The boundary between compile time and run time has always been somewhat artificial. A compiler runs at compile time, but it may embed information in the generated code that is only used at run time. A JIT compiler runs at run time, but it performs many of the same tasks as a static compiler. Macro systems blur the boundary further: code written by the programmer is transformed before it is compiled, effectively extending compile time.

\emph{Staging} is the deliberate, principled management of this boundary. A staging controller determines which computations happen at compile time and which at run time. The goal is to maximize the work done at compile time (specialization, optimization, verification) while preserving the flexibility of run-time computation.

This chapter examines the many ways in which modern programming languages and systems blur the compile-time / run-time boundary: from runtime code generation and JIT compilation, through template metaprogramming and constexpr, to macros and multi-stage programming. We develop a unified framework for understanding staging and show how it applies across a wide range of systems.

\section{Blurring the Compile-Time / Run-Time Boundary}\label{sec:blurring-boundary}

\subsection{The Traditional Model}

In the traditional compilation model, the boundary between compile time and run time is sharp:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Compile time}: The compiler reads source code, performs analysis and optimization, and produces target code. All type checking, constant folding, and dead code elimination happens here.
  \item \textbf{Run time}: The target code is loaded and executed. The compiled program operates on run-time data, with no access to the compiler's internal state.
\end{enumerate}

This model is clean but wasteful. Many computations that could be performed at compile time (because their inputs are known) are deferred to run time, where they incur overhead on every execution.

\subsection{The Staged Model}

In the staged model, the boundary is permeable:

