\begin{definition}[Staging Controller]\label{def:staging-controller}
A \emph{staging controller} is a mechanism that determines which computations are performed at compile time and which at run time. It may be:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Static}: determined by the language design (e.g., macros, template metaprogramming).
  \item \emph{Dynamic}: determined by run-time information (e.g., JIT compilation, runtime code generation).
  \item \emph{Hybrid}: a combination of static and dynamic decisions (e.g., partial evaluation with profiling feedback).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{Staging Controllers}\label{sec:staging-controllers}

\subsection{Static Staging Controllers}

Static staging controllers make all staging decisions at compile time, based on information available in the source program:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Macros}: Transform source code before compilation. The macro expansion happens at compile time; the expanded code is compiled normally.
  \item \textbf{Template metaprogramming}: Computation at the type level happens at compile time; the resulting types and values are embedded in the compiled code.
  \item \textbf{Constexpr}: Functions marked as \texttt{constexpr} are evaluated at compile time when their arguments are known.
  \item \textbf{Compile-time function execution}: Languages like D, Zig, and Odin allow arbitrary functions to be called at compile time.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Dynamic Staging Controllers}

Dynamic staging controllers make staging decisions at run time, based on information gathered during execution:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{JIT compilation}: The JIT compiler decides which methods to compile based on profiling data (call frequency, type feedback).
  \item \textbf{Runtime code generation}: Programs generate and execute code at run time, using libraries like \texttt{libjit}, LLVM's ORC, or architecture-specific assemblers.
  \item \textbf{Runtime specialization}: The runtime system specializes code based on observed types, inlining monomorphic call sites and deoptimizing when assumptions are violated.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Hybrid Staging Controllers}

The most sophisticated systems combine static and dynamic staging:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Tiered compilation}: Code is first interpreted, then compiled with minimal optimization (tier 1), then recompiled with aggressive optimization (tier 2) based on profiling data.
  \item \textbf{Multi-stage programming}: The programmer explicitly annotates staging decisions, and the system enforces them at compile time while allowing dynamic code generation at run time.
  \item \textbf{Partial evaluation with profiling}: The partial evaluator uses profiling data to guide specialization decisions, combining static analysis with dynamic information.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Runtime Code Generation}\label{sec:runtime-codegen}

\subsection{The Mechanism}

Runtime code generation (RCG) is the process of creating executable machine code during program execution. The basic mechanism involves:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Code construction}: Building a sequence of machine instructions (or an IR that is translated to machine instructions).
  \item \textbf{Memory allocation}: Allocating executable memory (using \texttt{mmap} with \texttt{PROT\_EXEC} on Unix, or \texttt{VirtualAlloc} with \texttt{PAGE\_EXECUTE} on Windows).
  \item \textbf{Code emission}: Writing the machine instructions to the executable memory.
  \item \textbf{Execution}: Calling the generated code as a function.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Libraries and Frameworks}

Several libraries support runtime code generation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{libjit}: A portable library for building and executing JIT-compiled code. Supports multiple architectures.
  \item \textbf{LLVM ORC}: LLVM's On-Request Compilation framework, which provides lazy JIT compilation with symbol resolution.
  \item \textbf{GNU Lightning}: A small, fast library for generating assembly code at run time. Designed for emulators and language implementations.
  \item \textbf{asmjit}: A lightweight, cross-platform JIT assembly compiler for x86/x64 and ARM.
  \item \textbf{dynasm}: A dynamic assembler that allows writing assembly code inline in C programs using a preprocessor.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Safety Considerations}

Runtime code generation raises significant safety concerns:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Memory safety}: Executable memory must be properly managed to prevent code injection attacks.
  \item \textbf{Type safety}: Generated code must be type-correct. Without type checking, generated code can cause undefined behavior.
  \item \textbf{Resource management}: Executable memory is a limited resource; leaked code can exhaust available memory.
  \item \textbf{Portability}: Machine-specific code must be generated for each target architecture.
\end{enumerate}

\section{JIT as Staging}\label{sec:jit-as-staging}

\subsection{The JIT Compiler as a Staging Controller}

A JIT compiler is, in essence, a staging controller that operates at run time. It observes the program's behavior and decides which parts to compile, when to compile them, and what optimizations to apply.

\begin{definition}[JIT as Staging]\label{def:jit-staging}
A JIT compiler performs the following staging decisions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{What to compile}: Which methods or traces to compile based on profiling data.
  \item \item \textbf{When to compile}: The threshold of invocations or iterations before compilation is triggered.
  \item \textbf{How to compile}: The level of optimization, based on the expected benefit and compilation cost.
  \item \textbf{When to recompile}: When profiling data changes (phase behavior), the compiled code is replaced with a new version.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Profiling as Compile-Time Information}

In the JIT context, profiling data serves the same role as static analysis in a traditional compiler: it provides information about the program's behavior that guides optimization.

\begin{example}[Type Specialization via JIT]\label{ex:jit-type-spec}
Consider a dynamically-typed language where a method is called with different argument types:
\begin{verbatim}
  def add(a, b) = a + b
\end{verbatim}
The JIT compiler observes that \texttt{add} is called 1000 times with integer arguments and 5 times with string arguments. It generates specialized code for the integer case (using machine integer addition) and falls back to the generic implementation for strings. This is \emph{type specialization}: a compile-time optimization driven by run-time profiling data.
\end{example}

\subsection{Tiered Compilation as Multi-Stage Staging}

Modern JIT compilers use \emph{tiered compilation}, which is a form of multi-stage staging:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Tier 0 (interpretation)}: Code is interpreted. Profiling data is collected.
  \item \textbf{Tier 1 (baseline compilation)}: Frequently executed code is compiled with minimal optimization. Fast compilation, moderate performance.
  \item \textbf{Tier 2 (optimizing compilation)}: Hot code is recompiled with aggressive optimization, using profiling data. Slow compilation, high performance.
  \item \textbf{Tier 3 (speculative optimization)}: The most critical code is compiled with speculative assumptions (e.g., ``this method always returns an integer''). If assumptions are violated, the code is deoptimized and recompiled at a lower tier.
\end{enumerate}

Each tier represents a different staging decision: more work is invested in compilation for code that is expected to benefit most from optimization.

\section{Template Metaprogramming (C++)}\label{sec:template-metaprogramming}

\subsection{Templates as a Compile-Time Language}

C++ templates constitute an accidentally Turing-complete programming language that runs at compile time. Template metaprogramming (TMP) exploits this to perform computations during compilation.

\begin{example}[Compile-Time Factorial in C++]\label{ex:cpp-tmp-factorial}
\begin{verbatim}
  template<int N>
  struct Factorial {
      static const int value = N * Factorial<N-1>::value;
  };

  template<>
  struct Factorial<0> {
      static const int value = 1;
  };

  // Usage: Factorial<5>::value is 120, computed at compile time
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{The Staging Interpretation}

Template metaprogramming can be understood as a staging mechanism:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Compile-time stage}: Template instantiation, type computation, and constexpr evaluation happen at compile time.
  \item \textbf{Run-time stage}: The instantiated templates produce types and constants that are embedded in the compiled code.
  \item \textbf{Staging annotations}: The \texttt{template} keyword marks code that runs at compile time; the absence of \texttt{template} marks run-time code.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations of C++ TMP}

C++ template metaprogramming has significant limitations as a staging mechanism:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Poor error messages}: Template errors are notoriously difficult to understand.
  \item \textbf{Limited expressiveness}: The template language lacks many features of a general-purpose language (prior to C++20 \texttt{consteval}).
  \item \textbf{Compilation time}: Complex template metaprograms can dramatically increase compilation time.
  \item \textbf{No run-time interaction}: Template computations cannot depend on run-time values.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Constexpr and Compile-Time Evaluation}\label{sec:constexpr}

\subsection{The Constexpr Model}

Modern C++ (C++11 through C++20) provides \texttt{constexpr} as a principled staging mechanism:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{constexpr} functions can be evaluated at compile time when their arguments are constant expressions.
  \item \texttt{consteval} functions (C++20) \emph{must} be evaluated at compile time.
  \item \texttt{constinit} variables (C++20) are initialized at compile time but can be modified at run time.
  \item \texttt{if constexpr} performs compile-time branching, eliminating dead branches from the compiled code.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Constexpr Compile-Time Computation]\label{ex:constexpr}
\begin{verbatim}
  constexpr int fibonacci(int n) {
      if (n <= 1) return n;
      return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
  }

  // Evaluated at compile time:
  constexpr int fib10 = fibonacci(10);  // 55

  // Can also be called at run time:
  int fib_n = fibonacci(user_input);
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Constexpr as a Staging Controller}

The \texttt{constexpr} model is a \emph{dual-use} staging controller: a function can be called at either compile time or run time, depending on the context. This is more flexible than the strict staging of multi-stage programming but less predictable.

\begin{definition}[Dual-Use Staging]\label{def:dual-use-staging}
A \emph{dual-use staging controller} allows a computation to be performed at either compile time or run time, depending on whether its inputs are known at compile time. This is in contrast to \emph{strict staging}, where the staging is determined by explicit annotations.
\end{definition}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Giving Required and Optional Evaluation Different Outcomes}

The same expression can be a compulsory constant in one context and
an ordinary computation in another. A staging controller should
distinguish that contextual requirement from the evaluator's ability
to make progress. If an optional fold hits a budget limit, the
original expression can still run. A required constant that hits the
limit needs a diagnostic rather than silent deferral.

\textbf{Contract.} In a toy exact-integer language, lower a pure,
typed expression in a required-constant or an optional-fold context.
A bounded evaluator returns a value, a runtime dependency, or
exhausted fuel. Any value is exact and independent of runtime inputs.
Fuel counts interpreter steps, and the residual fallback is the
original expression. This controller illustrates staging policy,
not the full constant-expression rules of a particular language.

\begin{sudocode}[Separate evaluation from context policy]
\Let{(status,v)}{\Call{Evaluate}{e,\mathrm{staticEnv},fuel}}
\If{$status=\mathrm{value}$}
  \To{Only a completed evaluation becomes a literal.}
  \State \Return $\mathrm{Literal}(v)$
\EndIf
\If{$context=\mathrm{required}$}
  \If{$status=\mathrm{runtimeDependency}$}
    \State \Return diagnostic: requires runtime input
  \EndIf
  \State \Return diagnostic: evaluation limit reached
\EndIf
\To{An optional fold must not become a new compile error.}
\State \Return $\mathrm{Residual}(e)$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Let the evaluator charge one step per expression
node. With fuel \(3\), \(6+7\) visits the addition and both
literals, so either context yields literal \(13\). With fuel
\(2\), the required context emits the limit diagnostic; the
optional context retains \(6+7\), which yields \(13\) at run
time. With fuel \(3\) and an unbound runtime input \(x\),
\(6+x\) in the required context instead reports that dependency.

The lowering invariant is that a successful optional fold has
the same runtime value as the original. No partial evaluation tree
is installed after a timeout. Keeping the two failure statuses
distinct helps users decide whether to change the input dependency
or raise an implementation limit. An actual language with effects
would also need rules identifying which evaluations the controller
is allowed to attempt.

\subsection{Compile-Time Evaluation in Other Languages}

Several other languages support compile-time evaluation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{D}: Supports compile-time function execution (CTFE) with the \texttt{enum} and \texttt{static} keywords.
  \item \textbf{Zig}: Allows arbitrary comptime evaluation using the \texttt{comptime} keyword.
  \item \textbf{Nim}: Supports compile-time execution via the \texttt{static} keyword and \texttt{const} expressions.
  \item \textbf{Rust}: Supports const evaluation via \texttt{const fn} and \texttt{const} contexts, with increasing capabilities in each release.
  \item \textbf{Julia}: The \texttt{@generated} macro allows functions to generate specialized code based on the types of their arguments.
\end{itemize}

\section{Macros as Staging}\label{sec:macros-staging}

\subsection{Macro Expansion as Compile-Time Computation}

Macro systems are among the oldest staging mechanisms. A macro transforms source code at compile time, before the main compilation proceeds. This is a form of staging: the macro runs at compile time, and its output is compiled as part of the main program.

\begin{definition}[Macro as Staging]\label{def:macro-staging}
A \emph{macro} is a compile-time function that takes source code (or AST fragments) as input and produces source code (or AST fragments) as output. The macro runs at compile time; its output is compiled and executed at run time.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Hygienic vs.\ Unhygienic Macros}

The distinction between hygienic and unhygienic macros is crucial for staging:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Unhygienic macros} (C preprocessor, early Lisp): The macro's output is spliced into the surrounding code without regard for variable binding. This can cause variable capture, where a macro's local variable accidentally binds a variable in the calling context.
  \item \textbf{Hygienic macros} (Scheme, Racket, Rust): The macro system ensures that variables introduced by the macro do not accidentally capture or shadow variables in the calling context. This is achieved by tracking the \emph{origin} of each identifier.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Macros in Modern Languages}

\begin{description}
  \item[Rust] Rust's \texttt{macro\_rules!} provides hygienic pattern-matching macros. Procedural macros allow arbitrary compile-time computation on token streams.
  \item[Racket] Racket's macro system is based on \emph{syntax transformers}: compile-time functions that operate on syntax objects. The system supports full hygiene, module-level macros, and multi-stage macros.
  \item[Scheme] Scheme's \texttt{syntax-rules} provides pattern-based hygienic macros. \texttt{syntax-case} adds the ability to inspect and decompose syntax objects.
  \item[Terra] Terra is a low-level systems language that is embedded in Lua. Lua code runs at compile time and generates Terra code, providing a powerful staging mechanism for systems programming.
\end{description}

\subsection{Macros and the Futamura Connection}

Macros are related to the Futamura projections in a subtle way. A macro system can be viewed as a partial evaluator that specializes the language to the macro-expanded form:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{macro\_expand}(m, p) = p'
\end{equation}

where $m$ is the macro definition, $p$ is the program using the macro, and $p'$ is the expanded program. This is analogous to the first Futamura projection: specializing the ``interpreter'' (the macro system) to the ``source program'' (the macro definition) produces a ``compiled program'' (the expanded code).

