\subsection{Design Principles for Staging}

Based on the survey of staging mechanisms, we can extract several design principles:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Type safety}: Staging should be type-safe. Generated code should be checked before execution.
  \item \textbf{Composability}: Staging constructs should be composable. Staged functions should be callable from other staged functions.
  \item \textbf{Predictability}: The staging decisions should be predictable. The programmer should be able to reason about what happens at compile time and what at run time.
  \item \textbf{Transparency}: The staging mechanism should be transparent to the run-time semantics. The staged program should produce the same results as the unstaged version.
  \item \textbf{Ergonomics}: The staging annotations should be lightweight and intuitive. Heavy annotation burdens discourage use.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Summary and Outlook}\label{sec:staging-summary}

The boundary between compile time and run time is increasingly porous. Modern programming languages and systems offer a rich variety of staging mechanisms, from compile-time evaluation (constexpr, template metaprogramming) through macro systems to runtime code generation and JIT compilation.

The key insights of this chapter are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Staging is a fundamental aspect of compiler design: every compiler makes staging decisions, whether explicitly or implicitly.
  \item The quality of staging decisions directly affects program performance: more work at compile time means less at run time.
  \item Type safety and composability are essential for practical staging: unsafe staging leads to bugs; non-composable staging leads to duplication.
  \item The trend is toward more sophisticated staging: JIT compilers, multi-stage programming, and compile-time evaluation are becoming standard features.
\end{enumerate}

The future of staging lies in the convergence of these mechanisms: compilers that combine static analysis, partial evaluation, and dynamic profiling to make optimal staging decisions automatically, while giving programmers the tools to override those decisions when necessary.

\section{Advanced Staging Techniques}\label{sec:advanced-staging}

\subsection{Multi-Stage Staging Controllers}

Some systems support \emph{multiple} staging controllers that interact:

\begin{example}[Layered Staging in a Web Framework]\label{ex:layered-staging}
A web framework might have three stages:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Design time}: Templates are checked for type correctness, routes are validated, and database schemas are verified.
  \item \textbf{Deploy time}: Configuration is resolved, database connections are established, and optimized code is generated for the target environment.
  \item \textbf{Run time}: Requests are processed, database queries are executed, and responses are generated.
\end{enumerate}
Each stage has its own staging controller, and information flows from earlier stages to later ones.
\end{example}

\subsection{Staging and Incremental Computation}

Staging is closely related to \emph{incremental computation}: the idea that when inputs change, only the affected outputs should be recomputed. In the staging context:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Compile-time caching}: Results of compile-time computations are cached and reused across compilations.
  \item \textbf{Incremental compilation}: When source code changes, only the affected parts are recompiled.
  \item \textbf{Incremental JIT}: When profiling data changes, only the affected compiled code is recompiled.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Staging for Domain-Specific Optimization}

Staging is particularly powerful for domain-specific optimization:

\begin{example}[Staging for Linear Algebra]\label{ex:staging-linalg}
A linear algebra DSL might use staging to:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item At compile time: determine the sizes of matrices, the sparsity patterns, and the target hardware.
  \item Generate specialized code: for known matrix sizes, generate unrolled loops; for sparse matrices, generate code that skips zeros; for GPU targets, generate CUDA kernels.
  \item At run time: the generated code operates on the actual data with no interpretation overhead.
\end{enumerate}
This is the approach taken by LMS-based systems like OptiML and Delite.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Cost Model for Staging Decisions}

A fundamental question is: \emph{how much work should be done at compile time?} The answer depends on a cost model:

\begin{definition}[Staging Cost Model]\label{def:staging-cost}
Let $C_c$ be the cost of compile-time computation and $C_r$ be the cost of run-time computation. A staging decision is \emph{optimal} if it minimizes:
\[
\alpha \cdot C_c + \beta \cdot C_r
\]
where $\alpha$ is the weight for compilation cost (important for fast build times) and $\beta$ is the weight for execution cost (important for performance-critical applications).

For code that is executed $N$ times, the break-even point is when $C_c = N \cdot (C_r^{\mathit{unstaged}} - C_r^{\mathit{staged}})$.
\end{definition}

This cost model explains why JIT compilers use thresholds: they wait until a method has been called enough times to justify the cost of compilation.

\section{Staging in Modern Language Implementations}\label{sec:staging-modern}

\subsection{Julia's Generated Functions}

Julia's \texttt{@generated} macro provides a powerful staging mechanism:

\begin{verbatim}
  @generated function process(x::T) where T
      if T <: Integer
          return :(x + 1)
      elseif T <: AbstractFloat
          return :(x * 2.0)
      else
          return :(error("unsupported type"))
      end
  end
\end{verbatim}

The body of the \texttt{@generated} function runs at compile time, with access to the types of the arguments. It returns an expression that is then compiled and executed at run time. This is a form of type-driven staging.

\subsection{Zig's Comptime}

Zig provides \texttt{comptime} as a first-class staging mechanism:

\begin{verbatim}
  fn fibonacci(comptime n: u32) u32 {
      if (n <= 1) return n;
      return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
  }

  const fib10 = comptime fibonacci(10);  // computed at compile time
\end{verbatim}

In Zig, \texttt{comptime} is not a special annotation but a fundamental part of the language: any function can be called at compile time, and the compiler evaluates it using the same interpreter that runs at run time. This eliminates the distinction between compile-time and run-time computation.

\subsection{Rust's Const Generics and Const Evaluation}

Rust is progressively expanding its compile-time evaluation capabilities:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{const fn}: Functions that can be evaluated at compile time.
  \item \textbf{const generics}: Type-level values that are known at compile time.
  \item \textbf{const evaluation}: The compiler's constant evaluator, which is a MIR interpreter.
  \item \textbf{proc macros}: Procedural macros that run at compile time and generate code.
\end{itemize}

The combination of these features makes Rust a powerful staging platform: generic code is specialized at compile time, const evaluation performs compile-time computation, and proc macros enable arbitrary code generation.

\section{Summary of Staging Principles}\label{sec:staging-principles-summary}

The study of staging mechanisms across languages and systems reveals several unifying principles:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Staging is universal}: Every compiler makes staging decisions, whether explicitly or implicitly. The question is not \emph{whether} to stage, but \emph{how} and \emph{when}.
  \item \textbf{Type safety matters}: Staging mechanisms that are type-safe (MSP, constexpr) are more reliable than those that are not (C preprocessor, runtime code generation).
  \item \textbf{Composability enables reuse}: Staging constructs that can be composed (macros, staged functions) are more powerful than those that cannot.
  \item \textbf{Profiling guides staging}: JIT compilers demonstrate that run-time profiling data can guide staging decisions more effectively than static analysis alone.
  \item \textbf{The boundary is blurring}: The trend is toward systems that seamlessly combine compile-time and run-time computation, rather than maintaining a sharp boundary.
\end{enumerate}

These principles will guide the design of future staging mechanisms, as languages continue to evolve toward more sophisticated, more efficient, and more flexible compilation strategies.
